        <mxCell id="note_redshift_rationale" parent="data-model" style="shape=note;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=1;fillColor=#FFFDE7;strokeColor=#FBC02D;fontColor=#000000;" value="Senior Architect Rationale for Redshift Serverless:&#xa;1. Complex correlation queries benefit from columnar storage + MPP&#xa;2. QuickSight performance: direct Redshift connection (low latency)&#xa;3. Materialized views: pre-aggregate heavy queries for instant dashboards&#xa;4. Serverless: no cluster ops, auto-scales, pay-per-use" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="150" width="520" x="640" y="320" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="note_glue_benefits" parent="data-model" style="shape=note;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=1;fillColor=#E8F5E9;strokeColor=#388E3C;fontColor=#000000;" value="Senior Data Architect Recommendations - Why AWS Glue for MongoDB → Analytics&#xa;&#xa;Compared Options (for ETL / Integration Layer):&#xa;- Custom EMR / Spark Jobs: Flexible but higher DevOps overhead, harder for new BI devs&#xa;- Lambda + Custom Code: Good for event-based small payloads, not ideal for large batch ETL&#xa;- Third-party ETL Tools: Extra licensing, less native AWS integration&#xa;&#xa;Why AWS Glue is Recommended Here:&#xa;1. Serverless ETL with native MongoDB connector → no cluster management&#xa;2. Integrated Data Catalog used by Athena, Redshift, and Glue jobs&#xa;3. Built-in support for schema inference and nested JSON/BSON flattening&#xa;4. Orchestration via Glue Workflows + EventBridge, with CloudWatch monitoring&#xa;5. Tight integration with S3 and Redshift COPY → simpler, end-to-end AWS pipeline" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="210" width="520" x="1180" y="320" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
